<h1>Knowledge Article: MECM Client Policy Reset and Client Reinstall</h1>
<p class="meta">Audience: Local Administrators &middot; Last updated: 13 November 2026</p>

<h2>Purpose</h2>
<p>This article gives Local Administrators two ways to recover a device whose MECM (Configuration Manager) client is not receiving software updates or not communicating with the site.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Option 1, reset client policy:</strong> run the script in this article. It takes about 7 minutes, needs no restart and is safe to run on a healthy client. Try this first.</li>
  <li><strong>Option 2, reinstall the client:</strong> use this only if Option 1 does not fix the device.</li>
</ul>
<p>Background: following the MECM policy signing incident in October and November 2026, some clients are holding out-of-date policy locally. The client does not clear this on its own, so it has to be reset.</p>

<h2>When to use this article</h2>
<p>Use it when a device shows one or more of these symptoms:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Software updates are not appearing, or not installing, in Software Center.</li>
  <li>Applications or updates sit at "waiting" or 0% and never download.</li>
  <li>Software Center is empty or missing items it normally shows.</li>
  <li>The Configuration Manager control panel Actions tab lists only one or two actions.</li>
  <li>The device shows as inactive in the MECM console, or has no recent policy request or heartbeat.</li>
  <li><code>C:\Windows\CCM\Logs\PolicyAgent.log</code> shows errors about a policy signature or hash failing verification.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Before you start</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Rights:</strong> you need local administrator rights on the device.</li>
  <li><strong>Network:</strong> the device must be on the network and able to reach the MECM management point.</li>
  <li><strong>Time:</strong> the script runs for about 7 minutes. Do not close the window while it runs.</li>
  <li><strong>Impact:</strong> no restart is needed and the user can keep working. The client downloads all of its policy again, so deployments can take 30 to 60 minutes to reappear in Software Center.</li>
  <li><strong>Record:</strong> note the device name (run <code>hostname</code>) in case you need to escalate.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Option 1: Reset client policy</h2>
<p>The script clears the client's local policy, requests a fresh copy from the site, then starts a software update scan and evaluation.</p>
<ol>
  <li>Sign in to the device with a local administrator account.</li>
  <li>Open the Start menu, type <strong>PowerShell</strong>, right-click <strong>Windows PowerShell</strong> and choose <strong>Run as administrator</strong>.</li>
  <li>Copy the whole script below, paste it into the window and press Enter. If it stops at the last line, press Enter once more.</li>
  <li>Wait about 7 minutes. The window shows nothing until the script finishes.</li>
  <li>Read the single line of output, then go to "Check the result".</li>
</ol>
<p>Paste the script directly into the window. A saved <code>.ps1</code> file may be blocked by the device's script execution policy.</p>

<pre><code># Runs one client action and records whether it succeeded
function Invoke-ClientAction {
    param(
        [string]$Name,
        [string]$Method,
        [hashtable]$Arguments
    )

    try {
        $r = Invoke-CimMethod -Namespace 'root\ccm' -ClassName 'SMS_Client' `
                              -MethodName $Method -Arguments $Arguments -ErrorAction Stop

        if ($null -ne $r.ReturnValue -and $r.ReturnValue -ne 0) {
            return "$Name=FAILED (return $($r.ReturnValue))"
        }
        return "$Name=OK"
    }
    catch {
        return "$Name=FAILED ($($_.Exception.Message.Trim()))"
    }
}

$results = @()

# 1. Purge local policy
$results += Invoke-ClientAction -Name 'Reset' -Method 'ResetPolicy' -Arguments @{ uFlags = [uint32]1 }
Start-Sleep -Seconds 60

# 2. Machine policy retrieval
$results += Invoke-ClientAction -Name 'Policy' -Method 'TriggerSchedule' -Arguments @{ sScheduleID = '{00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000021}' }
Start-Sleep -Seconds 180

# 3. Software updates scan
$results += Invoke-ClientAction -Name 'Scan' -Method 'TriggerSchedule' -Arguments @{ sScheduleID = '{00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000113}' }
Start-Sleep -Seconds 120

# 4. Software updates deployment evaluation
$results += Invoke-ClientAction -Name 'Eval' -Method 'TriggerSchedule' -Arguments @{ sScheduleID = '{00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000108}' }

# One line of output per client
$results -join '; '</code></pre>

<p>What each step does:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Step</th><th>Action</th><th>Wait afterwards</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Reset</td><td>Clears all locally held policy</td><td>60 seconds</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Policy</td><td>Requests a full set of policy from the site</td><td>180 seconds</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Scan</td><td>Starts a software updates scan</td><td>120 seconds</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Eval</td><td>Evaluates software update deployments</td><td>None</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Check the result</h2>
<p>A successful run prints this line:</p>
<pre><code>Reset=OK; Policy=OK; Scan=OK; Eval=OK</code></pre>

<table>
  <tr><th>Output</th><th>What it means</th><th>What to do</th></tr>
  <tr>
    <td>All four show <code>OK</code></td>
    <td>The client accepted every request</td>
    <td>Confirm recovery using the checks below</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><code>Reset=FAILED</code>, or all four failed</td>
    <td>The window was not run as administrator, or the client is not installed or not responding</td>
    <td>Run it again in an administrator window. If it fails again, go to Option 2</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Only <code>Scan</code> or <code>Eval</code> failed</td>
    <td>Policy had not finished arriving when the step ran</td>
    <td>Wait 10 minutes, then run <strong>Software Updates Scan Cycle</strong> and <strong>Software Updates Deployment Evaluation Cycle</strong> from the control panel Actions tab</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<p><code>OK</code> means the client accepted the request. It does not confirm the scan or evaluation finished successfully, so check the device 30 to 60 minutes later:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Open Control Panel &gt; <strong>Configuration Manager</strong> &gt; <strong>Actions</strong>. A recovered client lists a full set of actions, not just one or two.</li>
  <li>Open <strong>Software Center</strong> and confirm updates and applications are listed and can download.</li>
  <li>Open <code>C:\Windows\CCM\Logs\PolicyAgent.log</code> and confirm there are no new signature or hash errors after the time you ran the script.</li>
</ol>
<p>If the device has not recovered after 60 minutes, go to Option 2.</p>

<h2>Option 2: Reinstall the client</h2>
<p>Use this when Option 1 fails, or when the device still cannot get software updates or communicate with the site an hour after a successful reset. A clean reinstall removes the client and everything it holds locally, then installs it fresh.</p>

<h3>Before you start</h3>
<p>The reinstall is run from the MECM console on COR TSP, so nothing needs to be run on the device itself. You need access to the console and rights to install clients. The device must be powered on and connected to the network.</p>

<h3>Steps</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Sign in to COR TSP and open the <strong>Configuration Manager console</strong>.</li>
  <li>Go to <strong>Assets and Compliance</strong> &gt; <strong>Devices</strong>.</li>
  <li>Type the device name in the search box and press Enter.</li>
  <li>Confirm you have the right device. If the name appears more than once, choose the record with the most recent activity.</li>
  <li>Right-click the device and select <strong>Install Client</strong>.</li>
  <li>Select <strong>Next</strong> on the first page of the wizard.</li>
  <li>On the <strong>Installation Options</strong> page, tick both of these:
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Always install the client software</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Uninstall existing Configuration Manager client before the client is installed</strong></li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li>If asked, select <strong>Install the client software from a specified site</strong> and choose your site.</li>
  <li>Select <strong>Next</strong>, <strong>Next</strong>, then <strong>Close</strong>.</li>
  <li>Wait 20 to 30 minutes, then refresh the device in the console. A successful reinstall shows <strong>Client: Yes</strong>, <strong>Client Activity: Active</strong> and a current policy request time.</li>
  <li>On the device, repeat the three checks in "Check the result". You do not need to run the Option 1 script afterwards.</li>
</ol>
<p>Notes:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The uninstall option is what clears the out-of-date policy. Without it, the client is repaired in place and the problem can remain.</li>
  <li>If the uninstall option is not shown in the wizard, stop and escalate.</li>
  <li>The uninstall can clear the client's download cache, so content already downloaded will be fetched again.</li>
  <li>If the device has not changed in the console after 60 minutes, escalate. The push may not have reached the device.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Escalation</h2>
<p>If the device is still not working after Option 2, raise a DSMS ticket to <span class="todo">[ASSIGNMENT GROUP TO BE CONFIRMED]</span> and include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Device name and location.</li>
  <li>Date and time each option was attempted.</li>
  <li>The output line from the Option 1 script.</li>
  <li>The symptoms still present.</li>
  <li>Copies of these logs:
    <ul>
      <li><code>C:\Windows\CCM\Logs\PolicyAgent.log</code></li>
      <li><code>C:\Windows\CCM\Logs\ClientIDManagerStartup.log</code></li>
      <li><code>C:\Windows\CCM\Logs\LocationServices.log</code></li>
      <li><code>C:\Windows\CCM\Logs\CcmMessaging.log</code></li>
      <li><code>C:\Windows\ccmsetup\Logs\ccmsetup.log</code></li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</ul>

